    "result_text": "The build is green. There's only one test class, and the full suite passes.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country national-number length validation in the E.164 telephone normalizer.\n\n**Change** \u2014 `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/util/TelephoneNormalizer.java`:\n\n- Added a `NATIONAL_LENGTH_BY_COUNTRY_CODE` map (`+61` \u2192 9 national digits, `+1` \u2192 10), ordered longest-prefix-first so the country code matches greedily.\n- Added a `hasValidNationalLength(...)` helper that, after the existing generic 8\u201315 digit bound, checks the digits following a recognised country code against the exact required count. Unrecognised country codes fall back to the generic bound.\n- `normalize(...)` now returns `Optional.empty()` when the national length is wrong, so the controller's existing path (`normalizeTelephone` \u2192 `InvalidOwnerFieldsException`) rejects it with **400**.\n- Updated the class and method javadoc to document the rule.\n\n**How it satisfies the flow**: The new acceptance test submits `+61 123` (2 national digits for `+61`, which requires 9) and now gets 400. The valid fixtures still pass \u2014 `uniqueTelephone()` (`04XXXXXXXX` \u2192 `+61` + 9 national digits) and the `+1` NANP seed data (`+16085551023` \u2192 `+1` + 10 national digits) both meet their country's required length.\n\n**Tests**: `BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`.",
